Currently the setup is as follows:
First (and only) sacrifice gift:
-The Marauder's Map
--Is a scroll of Magic Mapping that is not consumed when read, and can be r=
epeatedly read with no timeout.
--Constantly detects monsters, as "oESP.
--Confers automatic searching.
--When invoked, casts Detect Objects, followed by Detect Artifacts, with th=
e standard timeout after invocation.

__________________________________________________________________

All very nice powers (especially free magic mapping), but it comes at the e=
xpense of easy access to good melee artifacts. As has been pointed out, fre=
e magic mapping isn't much different than what a spellcaster with a spellbo=
ok of Magic Mapping can do.

Should I remove the timeout on Detect Objects/Artifacts? The power is desig=
ned to allow Pirates to find and take advantage of any artifacts that are r=
andomly generated as they descend through the dungeon. Limiting the frequen=
cy at which you can use this power seems to defeat that purpose.
__________________________________________________________________

Quest Artifact
-The Treasury of Proteus
-A cursed treasure chest. It is not yet implemented, but its planned powers=
 are:
--Reduced base weight (~that of an oilskin sack).
----Weight of it's contents remains unchanged. The reduction in base weight=
 is to allow the player to carry the chest without instantly becoming burde=
ned.
--Prevents contents from becoming wet
--Repeatedly polymorphs its contents while carried.
--While carried, attracts curses, protecting your inventory at the expense =
of the chest's contents.
--While carried, absorbs enemy magic spells (ie, provides magic resistance)=
.
--While carried, causes perhaps 1/4 of randomly generated monsters to be re=
placed by other pirates attempting to steal the chest. These pirates will a=
ttack nearby monsters as well as the player.
__________________________________________________________________

Another useful utility item. The polymorph effect is essentially a very slo=
w polypile effect that is limited to the total number of items you can carr=
y. If too many items are placed within the chest, some may be destroyed by =
the polymorph effect (similar to golem formation).
__________________________________________________________________

Crowning Artifact
(Pirates are crowned "Pirate King" regardless of alignment, and receive Rea=
ver instead of the default crowning artifact)
-Reaver
--+1d4 to-hit
--+1d8 damage
--Steals one item from the target with each successful attack.
__________________________________________________________________

This is the Pirate's guaranteed artifact weapon, but comes with the major d=
rawback of a greatly increased prayer timeout.

Should I disable wishing for this artifact? Automatic theft is a pretty pow=
erful effect, since it allows you to get consumables away from monsters bef=
ore they have a chance to waste them (sure enough, one of the first things =
I stole during testing was an amulet of life saving). It does require dilig=
ent inventory management to avoid drowning in junk.

Perhaps I should disable wishing for Pirates only, but give Reaver only a 1=
0% chance of stealing when wielded by other classes?

